# The commons — why some fisheries collapse and some do not
#
# ==============================================================================
# WHAT IS REAL HERE, AND WHAT IS NOT
#
# The structure is documented; the emotional coefficients are a reading of it.
#
# The collapse case is the Grand Banks northern cod. Catches peaked above
# 800,000 tonnes in 1968; spawning biomass fell about 93% in thirty years, from
# 1.6 million tonnes in 1962 to between 72,000 and 110,000 in 1992; the
# moratorium was announced on 2 July 1992, put around 30,000 people out of work
# in Newfoundland and Labrador, and lasted 32 years rather than the expected two.
#
# The other case is the one standard models do not predict. Elinor Ostrom's work,
# and James Acheson's ethnography of Maine's lobster harbour gangs, document
# common-pool resources that are *not* destroyed by the people using them. In
# Alanya, Turkey, fishers who had been fighting over the best spots in the early
# 1970s built a rotation: a list of who may fish, named locations, lots drawn each
# September, and everyone shifting one spot east per day. In Maine, harbour gangs
# hold informal territories with a graduated sanction ladder -- a word first, then
# interference with gear, then destruction of it, then exclusion.
#
# That ladder is the part worth stealing. It is not a policy anybody wrote down;
# it is escalation driven by accumulated grievance, which is the one thing this
# runtime computes natively.
#
# ==============================================================================
# WHY THIS NEEDS AN EMOTIONAL MODEL
#
# Ostrom, Walker and Gardner's laboratory experiments on common-pool resources
# found something a payoff matrix cannot explain: letting subjects talk raises
# cooperation, talk plus the ability to sanction raises it further, and -- the
# part that matters here -- *the effect persists after the talking stops*.
#
# That last finding is not a claim about incentives. It is a claim about memory,
# and it is the claim this language makes natively. `tests/test_semantics.py`
# turns it into an assertion: TestCommons checks that a group which has built
# relationships holds up better than an identical group of strangers, and that it
# still does after the relationships stop being maintained.
#
# Below are the same four fishers, the same boats and the same stock, run twice.
# The only difference between the two halves is whether they know each other.

group harbour

agent north south east west
join north harbour
join south harbour
join east harbour
join west harbour

# The stock everyone draws on, and what each of them needs to get by. Fish is an
# ordinary good, so it can be competed for -- which is exactly the point.
have north fish 3
have south fish 3
have east fish 1
have west fish 1

need north fish 2
need south fish 2
need east fish 3
need west fish 3

# The rotation is a shared undertaking that nobody is obliged to uphold.
goal harbour "keep the rotation"

# --- what the harbour has that a fleet of strangers does not -------------------
#
# Neighbours, an ethnographic fact rather than a policy: these people have known
# each other for years.

trust north south 0.7
trust south north 0.7
trust east west 0.65
trust west east 0.65
trust north east 0.5
trust east north 0.5
love south west 0.4
love west south 0.4

tick 25

observe harbour
observe east
observe north
